-- title:
--   Counting identity in the ordinary corner of J_H(M)[p]
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $J=J_H(M)$ denotes $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` of the modular curve of level $(M,H)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and $T_pJ$ denotes the Tate module [`TateModule p (JH M H)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15), i.e. the group of sequences $(x_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ in $J$ with $p^nx_n=0$ and $px_{n+1}=x_n$.
--
--   **Arithmetic data.** A prime $p$ with $p\neq 2$ (`hp2`); a natural number $M$ with $p\mid M$ (`hpM`) and $p^2\nmid M$ (`hpM2`); a subgroup $H\leq(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ such that every unit whose image under the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times\to(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is trivial already lies in $H$ (`hHp`); a set $S\subseteq\mathbb{N}$; and the hypothesis `hin` that `HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H` holds, i.e. the predicate `HeckeInputsHAlong` holds over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ at level $(M,H)$ for every prime $\ell$, and every $d\in(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ is realised by an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism of the function field satisfying `IsDiamondAutHBar`.
--
--   **Hecke block.** A commutative ring $\mathbb{T}$ that is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra and acts on $T_pJ$ compatibly with the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-structure; the action is faithful (`hfaith`: an element killing all of $T_pJ$ is zero); a map `op` from the generators [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (the symbols $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell\notin S$ with $\ell\nmid M$, $U_q$ for primes $q\mid M$, and the diamonds $\langle d\rangle$) to $\mathbb{T}$ whose elements act on $T_pJ$ as the corresponding operators `tateGenOpH` (`hop`), the range of `op` generating $\mathbb{T}$ as a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra (`hgen`); an idempotent splitting $S'$ of $\mathbb{T}$ in the sense of [`IharaLemma.IdempotentSplitting`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L7) (finitely many idempotents $e_i$ forming a complete orthogonal family, maximal ideals $\mathfrak{m}_i$ exhausting the maximal ideals of $\mathbb{T}$, with $e_i\in\mathfrak{m}_j$ if and only if $i\neq j$); and an index $i_0$ with $\mathrm{op}(U_p)\notin\mathfrak{m}_{i_0}$ (`hord`), the ordinarity condition at $i_0$.
--
--   **The place.** A valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $p$ in the sense that $p$ is a non-unit of $Pl$ (`hPl`), whose residue field has characteristic $p$ and is algebraically closed.
--
--   **Models and Néron objects.** The hypothesis `hj` that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the level-one $q$-expansion function field; an integral model datum $\mathfrak{X}$ of type `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`, comprising in particular a curve model `𝔛.Meta` of $\bar F_H(M)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ together with the isomorphism `𝔛.eeta` onto the geometric generic fibre; a level datum $\Lambda$ of type `LevelData p M H hpM Pl` (a section $\sigma_A$ of the base, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ with a morphism $\Lambda.f$ to the base, a relative group law $\Lambda.L$, and bijections $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$, $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ identifying $J_{H'}(M/p)$ and $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the mod-$\mathfrak{P}$ function field with the generic and special points of $\Lambda.f$); the hypothesis `hΛ` that $\Lambda.f$ satisfies the abelian-scheme property bundle (smooth, proper, connected fibres, and admitting a relative group law); and an object $O$ of type `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`, with underlying scheme $O.G$ over the base via $O.g$, relative group law $O.L$, bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}\colon J\to$ points of $O.g$ over the geometric generic point, Hecke correspondences $O.\mathrm{hecke}$, toric rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$, and the subgroups $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,m$ (generated by the $m$-torsion classes $x$ for which $O.\mathrm{pts}\,x$ satisfies the predicate `ExtendsToPlace` for $Pl$ and $\Lambda.\sigma_A$) and $O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,m$ (generated by the range of $O.\mathrm{toricPoint}\,m$ for $m>0$).
--
--   **Abel–Jacobi pinning block** (`hD`, `hDQ`, `hsep`, `ajQ`, `kQ`, `ajbar`, `εbar`, `hpoinc`, `hajQε`, `hajQ`, `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, `hpts_law`, `hAJ`, `hrepΛ`; their content is summarised here). The designation $(O.G,O.g,\text{unit section of }O.L)$ represents the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ subfunctor cut out by fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero, for the integral model `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ (`hD`) and for its base change to $\mathbb{Q}$ (`hDQ`), the latter morphism being separated (`hsep`); $ajQ$ is a morphism from the base-changed curve to the base-changed designation over $\mathbb{Q}$, carrying the $\varepsilon_{\inf}$-section to the zero section (`hajQε`), for which the two Poincaré bundles correspond (`hpoinc`) and which satisfies the Abel–Jacobi property `hajQ`: for every field $K$, every $K$-point $t$ of $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathbb{Q}$ and every $K$-point $x$ of the curve, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by $ajQ$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the $\varepsilon_{\inf}$-section. The morphism $kQ$ compares the fibre over the geometric generic point with the fibre over $\mathbb{Q}$, compatibly with both projections (`hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`); $\overline{aj}$ is the composite of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$, $kQ$, $ajQ$ and the first projection (`hajbar`) and lies over the generic point (`hajbar_over`); $\overline{\varepsilon}$ is a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of `𝔛.Meta.C` inducing $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ (`hεbar`) and sent by $\overline{aj}$ to the unit section (`hεbar_aj`). Moreover $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for the relative group law obtained from `hD` through `algEquivZeroGroupCut` (`hpts_law`), and `hAJ` pins $\overline{aj}$ on points: for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x,s$ of the curve model with $s$ inducing $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $(\text{place of }x)-(\text{place of }s)$ whose class under $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is $\overline{aj}$ precomposed with $x$. Finally `hrepΛ` asserts the analogous representability statement for the designation built from $\Lambda$ at level `ΓN p M H hpM`.
--
--   **The inertia ring.** A domain $R$ which is a henselian local ring with algebraically closed residue field, an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra with faithful scalar action, whose image lies in $Pl$ (`hRA`), which is a discrete valuation ring (`hRdvr`) in which $p$ is irreducible (`hRirr`), such that an automorphism $\sigma$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ lies in `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` exactly when it fixes the image of $R$ pointwise (`hRfix`), and such that every element of $Pl$ fixed by all of inertia lies in that image (`hRmax`).
--
--   **Finite-part layer.** A $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ over $R$ of height $h$ (levels $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, finite free Hopf algebras with surjective transition maps and $\mathrm{rank}=p^{vh}$), an additive map $\Delta\colon\mathcal{G}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})\to J$, and a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $e\colon T_p\mathcal{G}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})\to T_pJ$, subject to: injectivity of $\Delta$ (`hΔinj`); the level identification `hΔlev`, $y\in O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ if and only if $y=\Delta(x)$ for some level-$v$ point $x$; Galois equivariance `hΔgal` of $\Delta$ for automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ that are $R$-linear; Hecke compatibility `hΔhecke`, for every set of primes and every generator $g$ there is a family of $R$-coalgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ of the levels, compatible with the transitions, such that $\Delta$ intertwines precomposition by $\varphi_v$ with `genOpH` applied to $g$; the levelwise description `he` of $e$ in terms of $\Delta$, its injectivity (`heinj`), the description `herange` of its range (those Tate vectors whose $n$-th entry lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^n)$), its Galois equivariance `hegal` relating `tateModuleRep` and `JH.tateGaloisRep`, the saturation `hsat` of the range ($py$ in the range implies $y$ in the range), the hypothesis `hcoker` that the quotient $T_pJ/\mathrm{range}(e)$ is $\mathbb{Z}_p$-free of rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$, and `htor`, that toric points at each level lie in the image of $\Delta$.
--
--   **Toric quotient layer.** A $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ over $R$ of height $h_B$ with $h=O.\mathrm{toricRank}+h_B$ (`hhB`) and $h_B=2h'$ for some $h'$ (`hhB2`), together with levelwise maps $\psi_v\colon\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v\to\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ compatible with the transitions (`hψt`), such that a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$ restricts to the trivial point of $\mathcal{B}$ exactly when its image under $\Delta$ lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$ (`hψker`), every point of $\mathcal{B}$ is such a restriction (`hψsurj`), a point of $\mathcal{G}$ reduces to the identity whenever its restriction to $\mathcal{B}$ does — the reduction condition being that $Pl$-valuation of $\hat{x}(a)-\varepsilon(a)$ is $<1$ for all $a$, where $\hat x$ is the algebra map of the point and $\varepsilon$ the counit composed with the structure map (`hψred`) — and the period condition `hperiod`: for $\sigma$ in inertia, $z$ a $p^v$-torsion class of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of $\bar F_H(M)$ and $y$ a level-$v$ point with $\Delta(y)=\sigma z-z$, the restriction of $y$ to $\mathcal{B}$ reduces to the identity.
--
--   **Pinning of $\mathcal{G}$ inside $O.G$.** A ring homomorphism $\rho_h\colon R_p\to R$ and morphisms $\iota_v\colon\mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v)\to O.G$ such that: $\rho_h$ is compatible with the structure maps to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρh`); $\iota_v$ lies over the base through $\rho_h$ (`hιbase`), the induced map to the pullback being a closed immersion (`hιcl`); $\iota_v$ is killed by $p^v$ (`hιp`); $\iota_v$ computes $O.\mathrm{pts}\circ\Delta$ on points (`hιpts`); $\iota_v$ is multiplicative for the group law $O.L$ on points with values in any $R$-algebra (`hιmul`); it is compatible with the transition maps (`hιt`); and `hιhecke` provides, for every generator $g$, a family $\varphi_v$ as above with $\iota_v$ after $\mathrm{Spec}(\varphi_v)$ equal to $O.\mathrm{hecke}$ at $g$ after $\iota_v$, and intertwining $\Delta$ with `genOpH`. Finally `hιfin` states that the morphism $j_v$ induced by $\iota_v$ from $\mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v)$ into the base change along $\rho_h$ of the $p^v$-kernel of $O.L$ is both an open and a closed immersion, and that every point of that pullback lying over the closed point of $R$ is in the range of $j_v$.
--
--   **Atkin–Lehner block.** A semilinear automorphism `wgen` of $\bar F_H(M)$ whose action on places computes that of $\mathfrak{X}.w$ on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points (`hwgen`), an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism $\theta$ of $\bar F_H(M)$ acting on the $q$-expansions of functions coming from level $M/p$ by the substitution $q\mapsto q^p$ (`hθ`, via `qExpand`), and the identification `hwθ` of `wgen` with the semilinear automorphism attached to $\theta$.
--
--   **Degeneracy and special-fibre block.** With $M/p$ nonzero and principal divisors available for both function fields: the additivity of $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$ (`hΛpts_add`) and of $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ for the group law based changed to the residue point (`hΛptsSp_add`); two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra maps $\alpha_H,\beta_H$ from $\bar F_{H'}(M/p)$ to $\bar F_H(M)$, each integral, satisfying the fundamental identity, finite, and satisfying the norm formula for divisor pushforward (`hαHint`, `hαHFI`, `hαHfin`, `hαHN` and the four analogues for $\beta_H$); the descriptions `hdeg0`, `hdeg1` of $O.\mathrm{degPts}\,0$ and $O.\mathrm{degPts}\,1$ on classes as divisor pushforward along $\alpha_H$ and along $\beta_H$; endomorphisms $F,F^{-1},F^{*}$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the mod-$\mathfrak{P}$ function field, where $F$ is the $q$-expansion Frobenius pushforward `qExpFrobeniusPushforwardModL` at level `ΓN p M H hpM` (`hF`), $F^{-1}$ is a two-sided inverse of $F$ (`hFinv`) and $F^{*}=p\cdot F^{-1}$ (`hFstar`); a unit $pb$ of $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ reducing to $p$ (`hpb`) and the diamond endomorphism $\delta$ given by the semilinear action of `diamondActionModL` at the lift of $pb$ (`hδ`); a pair of additive maps $\alpha\mathrm{pull}_i\colon J_{H'}(M/p)\to J$ and a pair of morphisms $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ from $\Lambda.f$ to $O.g$ over the base, indexed by $i\in\{0,1\}$, compatible with points (`hpull`) and with the group laws (`hpull_mul`), such that on the special fibre the pair attached by `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair O.ssFinset` to the composite of a point $x$ with $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ is $(z,F^{*}z)$ for $i=0$ and $(F^{*}z,\delta z)$ for $i=1$, where $z=\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}(x)$ (`hpullsp`), and such that $\alpha\mathrm{pull}_0$ and $\alpha\mathrm{pull}_1$ are given on classes by divisor pullback along $\alpha_H$ and along $\beta_H$ respectively (`hpullα`, `hpullβ`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** Write $X\subseteq J$ for the image under the first-level projection [`TateModule.proj p (JH M H) 1`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L122) of the additive subgroup underlying the corner submodule $e_{i_0}\cdot T_pJ$ (the range of multiplication by the idempotent $S'.e\,i_0$ on $T_pJ$). Then, with all four cardinalities taken as `Set.ncard`,
--   $$\#\{x\in X:\ x\in O.\mathrm{finPts}\,p\}\cdot\#\{x\in X:\ x\in O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,p\}\ =\ \#A\cdot\#B,$$
--   where $A$ is the set of $x\in X$ for which there exists a level-one point $y$ of $\mathcal{G}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\Delta(y)=x$ and with $y$ reducing to the identity, that is, the $Pl$-valuation of $\hat y(a)-\varepsilon(a)$ is $<1$ for every $a\in\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,1$ (here $\hat y$ is the $R$-algebra map attached to $y$ and $\varepsilon$ the counit followed by the structure map $R\to\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$), and $B$ is the set of $x\in X$ with $x\in O.\mathrm{finPts}\,p$ which are inertia-cyclotomic: for every $\sigma\in$ `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` and every natural number $c$ such that $\sigma\zeta=\zeta^{c}$ for all $\zeta\in\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\zeta^{p}=1$, one has $\sigma\cdot x=c\cdot x$.
--
--   This is the numerical form of Cartier duality on the good-reduction part of the ordinary corner of $J_H(M)[p]$ at a place above $p$: the product of the orders of the finite and toric parts equals the product of the orders of the subgroup of classes coming from points of the $p$-divisible group that reduce to the identity and of the subgroup of inertia-cyclotomic classes in the finite part. It is combined with the inclusions between these four subgroups to yield the equivalence, for classes in the corner lying in the finite part, between reducing to the identity and being inertia-cyclotomic.
